// code does not implement. The page ships publicly in republish #1 (P34.17).
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="How SIG collects data" path="/data-collection/">
  <p data-copy="DC-02 DC-21">
    SIG — the Surveillance Infrastructure Graph — is an open, evidence-first
    record of public surveillance infrastructure, built by fetching public
    records. This page is the explanation the crawler's contact URL points to:
    what it fetches, how it identifies itself, the conduct policy it runs
    under, how it treats robots.txt, and how to ask it to stop.
  </p>

  <h2 data-copy="DC-04">What SIG fetches</h2>
  <p data-copy="DC-05 DC-06">
    SIG fetches public records only — pages, documents, datasets and APIs that
    a public body or a transparency project publishes openly. It never collects
    licence-plate reads, trip histories or other per-person data: those are
    excluded by design, not filtered out afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2 data-copy="DC-07">How the crawler identifies itself</h2>
  <p data-copy="DC-08 DC-09">
    Every request carries a User-Agent header of the form{" "}
    <code>name/version (+https://surveillancegraph.org/data-collection/)</code>,
    where <code>name</code> is the connector doing the fetch — for example{" "}
    <code>osm/1.0.0</code> or the project fallback <code>SIG</code>. SIG never
    impersonates a browser and never rotates or disguises its agent to get past
    a block.
  </p>

  <h2 data-copy="DC-22">The conduct policy</h2>
  <p data-copy="DC-23 DC-24">
    Eight rules bind every fetch SIG makes — the crawler conduct policy's text
    of record is a committed policy file, and this page carries it verbatim. A
    copy check fails the build when the text claims conduct the code does not
    implement.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li data-copy="CR-01">
      <strong>Identify.</strong> A descriptive UA with a contact URL and an
      explanation page. No spoofing. The contact URL names an owned explanation
      page on SIG's own domain
      (https://surveillancegraph.org/data-collection/), never the operator's
      personal identifiers (ADR-168).
    </li>
    <li data-copy="CR-02">
      <strong>Probe and record robots.txt.</strong> Probe and record
      robots.txt, including AI-crawler directives and content-signal headers.
      The per-host verdict is classified per RFC 9309 §2.3.1.4 (retrieved /
      no_policy_4xx / unretrievable) and recorded on the fetch record
      (ADR-087). Under the operator's GL-GATE-08 disposition
      (ADR-088; re-confirmed A-5 and applied to every host — ADR-168) a
      disallowed or unretrievable verdict does not gate the fetch: it proceeds
      marked robots_disregarded, and each disregard is disclosed publicly as
      host + count with the decision reference. A disregarded disallow is
      never a rights reservation, a licence or an ingestion clearance;
      an affirmative machine-readable reservation is
      honoured as a refusal. Documented, allow-listed API endpoints run under
      ADR-083's API mode (robots not applied to the named endpoint; rate-limit
      and no-circumvention still apply). The binding access control is the
      fail-closed rights gate (ingestion_permitted), which robots does
      not replace.
    </li>
    <li data-copy="CR-03">
      <strong>Rate-limit conservatively.</strong> Rate-limit conservatively,
      per host, with backoff. Never burden a small civic host.
    </li>
    <li data-copy="CR-04">
      <strong>Never circumvent access controls.</strong> No authentication
      bypass, no paywall evasion, no challenge-solving, no proxy rotation or
      human-mimicking to defeat bot management. This is a legal posture;
      deviating from it is an ADR-level decision on the
      operator's recorded word, never a routine engineering choice (ADR-182).
    </li>
    <li data-copy="CR-05">
      <strong>Prefer the offered channel.</strong> If a source publishes an
      API, a bulk download, or a partner feed, use it instead of scraping the
      HTML.
    </li>
    <li data-copy="CR-06">
      <strong>Ask first.</strong> Ask first where the compact is unresolved and
      the source is a small civil-society project. Waived for
      DocumentCloud/MuckRock only (WV-09 / ADR-187); the rule binds every other
      source.
    </li>
    <li data-copy="CR-07">
      <strong>Honor opt-out.</strong> Honor opt-out immediately and record it
      in the compact. The host-level opt-out register
      (sig.opt-out-register/1) is checked before every fetch — before even the
      robots probe — and a listed host receives zero egress; an
      operator-supplied register file applies an entry on the next run, with
      no rebuild or redeploy (ADR-168).
    </li>
    <li data-copy="CR-08">
      <strong>Cache aggressively; refetch rarely.</strong> Conditional
      requests, content-hash short-circuits.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 data-copy="DC-10">How robots.txt is treated</h2>
  <p data-copy="DC-11 DC-12 DC-13 DC-25 DC-26">
    For every host it fetches, SIG retrieves and records the host's{" "}
    <code>robots.txt</code> and classifies the answer per RFC 9309. Under a
    recorded operator decision (GL-GATE-08 — ADR-088 and ADR-168), a{" "}
    <code>Disallow</code>, or a policy that cannot be retrieved, does not by
    itself stop the fetch; the fetch is marked{" "}
    <code>robots_disregarded</code> in SIG's record, so the refusal stays
    visible instead of being silently bypassed. Robots.txt is not the access
    control: the binding check is a fail-closed rights review per source, and a
    source is never fetched before its rights record permits it. Every
    disregard is disclosed publicly as the host and a count, with a reference
    to the recorded decision — it is never silent. A robots.txt refusal is an
    access wish, not a rights reservation: a host that signals one is still
    refused, as the next section states.
  </p>

  <h2 data-copy="DC-27">Opt-outs and rights reservations</h2>
  <p data-copy="DC-28 DC-29">
    A site operator can opt out at any time: SIG keeps a host-level opt-out
    register that is checked before every fetch — before even the robots.txt
    probe — and a listed host receives no requests at all. An entry applies on
    the next run — the fetcher can be pointed at an updated register file
    without a rebuild or redeploy — and every opt-out is recorded with where it
    was received.
  </p>
  <p data-copy="DC-30 DC-31">
    A machine-readable rights reservation is honoured as a refusal: a{" "}
    <code>Content-Signal</code> directive such as <code>ai-train=no</code>, a{" "}
    <code>TDM-Reservation</code> header, an <code>X-Robots-Tag</code>{" "}
    <code>noai</code> or <code>noimageai</code> directive, or an EU DSM Article
    4 reservation in a page's markup ends the fetch — the bytes are never
    captured — and is recorded on the source's rights record. That refused
    state is kept distinct from an unresolved rights review — a refusal closes
    the conversation where an unresolved record invites it — and a source
    carrying a recorded reservation is refused by the ingestion gate outright.
  </p>

  <h2 data-copy="DC-14">Rate limits</h2>
  <p data-copy="DC-15 DC-16">
    SIG fetches slowly — at least one second between requests to one host by
    default, and longer wherever a published crawl-delay or a documented API
    budget demands it. An HTTP 429, 503 or 504 answer is met with bounded
    backoff and retry; a bot-management challenge is recorded, never defeated
    or worked around.
  </p>

  <h2 data-copy="DC-17">Ask SIG to stop</h2>
  <p data-copy="DC-18 DC-20 DC-32">
    To ask SIG to stop fetching a site you operate, open a request through this
    site's <a href="/dispute/">dispute channel</a>. The dispute page names the
    contact address and states its own operating status. A stop request is
    recorded on the opt-out register described above and takes effect on the
    next fetch run.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
